<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. A second,
       earlier one would win in the served HTML (browsers and crawlers take the first). *@
    <meta name="description" content="Bit.Bmage - browser-side image processing for Blazor: load, crop, resize, adjust, annotate, redact and export images on the user's device from strongly typed C#, with an editor component and a headless API." />

    @* What a link to the site looks like when it is shared. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Bmage" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Bmage - browser-side image processing for Blazor" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Crop, resize, adjust, annotate, redact and export images in the browser from C#. No server round trips, no base64, no JavaScript image library." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles, which also point the editor's --bmg-* custom properties at the site's
       theme so the editor follows the header's light/dark switch. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* The editor component's own stylesheet. It is the one thing the editor asks of its host, and
       only a host that uses the editor links it; the browser bridge script (bit-bmage.js) is
       imported on demand by the library and needs no tag of its own. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Bit.Bmage/bit-bmage-editor.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Bmage.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once
   the app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. Bmage's image work is
       C# running on the user's device, so none of it can happen during the prerender pass: every
       page is readable straight from the served HTML, and its drop zones, sliders and editors come
       alive once the runtime is interactive - Layout/MainLayout.razor's data-interactive says when. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
